<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">wounds</ansi>

Some blows leave a wound that healing can't mend at once. A wound holds back
part of your health: while you're wounded, healing stops at your
<ansi fg="yellow">wound limit</ansi>, not at your maximum. Your maximum health doesn't change.
With 16 health and a 3-point wound, regeneration, potions, and spells heal
you only to 13, until the wound is treated.

You and your companions take both kinds. Enemies take only light wounds,
which close when the fight ends, and a few creatures take none
(<ansi fg="command">help coordination</ansi>).

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Where wounds come from</ansi>

  <ansi fg="yellow">Lasting wounds</ansi>  A critical hit that gets through the armor leaves one. It
                  holds back half the hit's damage, rounded up. The hit's
                  brackets say so: <ansi fg="command">(critical hit, 6 damage, bleeding, wounded)</ansi>.
                  Lasting wounds stay after the fight, through logout and
                  restarts, until they are treated or rested away.
                  A foe 3 or more levels below you is no real threat: its
                  critical hit leaves no wound while you stay at half your
                  health or more, and below that only a light wound of a
                  quarter of the hit.
  <ansi fg="yellow">Light wounds</ansi>    A crushing blow (one hit of a quarter of your maximum
                  health or more), or a bleed that runs its full course,
                  leaves one. Light wounds close when the fight ends.

The weapon decides the wound:

  swords, axes, whips, claws, fists, and bites   a cut
  daggers, spears, arrows, and bolts             a puncture
  maces, clubs, and hammers                      a broken bone

However many wounds you carry, healing can always bring you to a quarter of
your maximum health. Your limit shows in <ansi fg="command">status</ansi> as <ansi fg="command">12/16 (limit 13)</ansi>,
in <ansi fg="command">heal</ansi>, and in the web client's vitals strip. A heal the limit held
back says so: <ansi fg="command">(1 healed, wound limit 13 of 16)</ansi>. Your company's healers
heal a wounded member only up to their limit.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Treating wounds</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">heal wounds</ansi>        after a fight, whoever and whatever can help tends
                     the company (<ansi fg="command">help heal</ansi>)
  <ansi fg="command">cast tend [who]</ansi>    a cleric closes 2 to 6 points of the worst wound
                     (Tend Wounds, 4 mana); it heals no health itself
  linen bandage      closes 3 points of a cut or a puncture; used up
  birch splint       closes 4 points of a broken bone; used up
  a physician        closes every wound, for gold (15 a wound at the
                     Waymark Inn in Dunmar)
  an inn stay        a completed night at an inn (<ansi fg="command">help inn</ansi>) knits every
                     wound of the company with you
  a camp rest        a completed rest (<ansi fg="command">help camp</ansi>) knits a broken bone
                     for each birch splint the company has, and a cut or
                     puncture for each linen bandage, using them up;
                     without them, a camp heals no wounds
  a surgeon's kit    packed for a camp rest (<ansi fg="command">help camp gear</ansi>), it has a healer
                     with mana left tend one lasting wound first; five uses

Bandages and splints are sold in the markets of Dunmar and the Old Kings
Road (<ansi fg="command">help market</ansi>). Death clears your wounds, and a companion raised
by <ansi fg="command">resurrect</ansi> comes back with none, at half health (<ansi fg="command">help readiness</ansi>).

See also: <ansi fg="command">help heal</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help statuses</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help health</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help combat</ansi>.
